<p>Databricks&rsquo; new Genie says all the right things about running continuously, and almost nothing about knowing when it&rsquo;s finished or what happens when it breaks. I haven&rsquo;t run it. This is a reading of the company&rsquo;s <a href="https://www.databricks.com/blog/new-genie-autonomous-ai-complex-enterprise-work">announcement</a>, not a test, so treat everything below as opinion about the claims and the design.</p>

<h2>What does it claim?</h2>

<p>The headline claim is that &ldquo;Genie is now an autonomous AI coworker that handles long-running tasks on your behalf.&rdquo; The post goes on to say that it &ldquo;stays on the job continuously, tracking progress, following up on missing inputs.&rdquo; Once a job is running, &ldquo;it can run on a schedule or respond to triggers,&rdquo; with the example trigger &ldquo;such as when a metric dips.&rdquo;</p>

<p>The examples are concrete. In one, Genie investigates an incident using GitHub and Jira changes, keeps the ticket fresh, and keeps the team updated &ldquo;until the issue is resolved.&rdquo; In another, it monitors incoming claims for fraud patterns and loss-ratio drift, then assembles a case file and routes it to underwriters when a segment moves outside guidelines. In a third, it runs marketing campaigns &ldquo;around the clock&rdquo; and proposes weekly budget changes for approval.</p>

<h2>What&rsquo;s the design?</h2>

<p>I like the control model. Admins decide which connectors Genie can use, including tools reached through the Model Context Protocol (MCP), the standard many agents use to call outside software. Admins also set the &ldquo;approval requirements,&rdquo; and &ldquo;business users choose what Genie can do autonomously within those limits.&rdquo; Admins can also set budgets and spending caps, and &ldquo;every action Genie takes is traced and auditable.&rdquo;</p>

<p>So an administrator sets a ceiling, and a user picks a level underneath it. I think that&rsquo;s the sensible shape, because approval becomes a setting rather than a property of the product. It&rsquo;s the same idea I praised in OpenAI&rsquo;s dots back in October (see <a href="/blog/dots-and-the-approval-dial">OpenAI&rsquo;s dots and the approval dial</a>); the dial is where the interesting design lives.</p>

<h2>Where does it land?</h2>

<p>Here&rsquo;s the trouble. Like dots, Genie isn&rsquo;t one level of the <a href="/maturity">Maturity Model</a>; it&rsquo;s whatever level you configure. Fine, but the announcement doesn&rsquo;t let you place it. A reader can&rsquo;t tell where the defaults sit, so you can&rsquo;t tell what you&rsquo;re committing to before you&rsquo;ve set anything.</p>

<p>The words are right. Continuous, schedule, triggers, traced; that&rsquo;s the vocabulary of something that runs without you watching. And <a href="/blog/duration-is-not-autonomy">duration is not autonomy</a>, so the vocabulary alone doesn&rsquo;t settle the question. By the <a href="/evaluation">Evaluation framework</a>, what separates a scheduled assistant from an agentic complete system is deciding that work is done and recovering when it fails.</p>

<h2>What&rsquo;s missing?</h2>

<p>The post doesn&rsquo;t say which actions need approval by default, or how approvals are delivered or time out. It says Genie &ldquo;checks in when it needs input or approval,&rdquo; but if nobody answers at 3 a.m., I can&rsquo;t tell what happens next. Does the job wait, skip, or proceed?</p>

<p>The post doesn&rsquo;t describe what happens when a task fails, errors or stalls. The only related behavior is &ldquo;following up on missing inputs.&rdquo; That&rsquo;s one failure mode, the polite one. A revoked connector, a schema change, or a Jira outage is a different kind of problem.</p>

<p>The post also doesn&rsquo;t define or verify &ldquo;done.&rdquo; &ldquo;Until the issue is resolved&rdquo; names the finish line without saying who decides it was crossed. Is it Genie, the person who opened the ticket, or a check against the system of record? I made the longer case in <a href="/blog/completion-determination">Completion determination is the hardest capability to build</a>, and this is the example I&rsquo;d point to. An agent that decides for itself that the incident is resolved has graded its own homework.</p>

<p>Finally, the post doesn&rsquo;t say whether task state survives a restart. What it describes is that &ldquo;personal memory retains your preferences and instructions.&rdquo; That&rsquo;s memory of you, not goal state. Knowing that you like summaries in bullet form doesn&rsquo;t tell a job it was halfway through a case file. This is the <a href="/blog/state-store-problem">state store problem</a> in miniature.</p>

<h2>Fair?</h2>

<p>Mostly, with a concession. A launch post isn&rsquo;t a spec, and the documentation may answer every question above. I&rsquo;d be glad to be wrong about that.</p>

<p>But the announcement chose to describe guardrails and audit in detail. Silence on failure and completion is also a choice, a choice of emphasis. The post says Genie &ldquo;takes responsibility for ongoing work,&rdquo; and responsibility is exactly the word that makes you ask what it does when the work goes wrong.</p>

<h2>What can you test?</h2>

<p>You don&rsquo;t need to take my reading or theirs on faith. Give it a scheduled job with a real dependency, and break one input midway, for instance by revoking a connector. Then watch. Who notices first, you or Genie? Does it stop, retry, or quietly carry on with stale data? And does it ever tell you it&rsquo;s done, and was it right?</p>

<p>The post says you can &ldquo;supervise, steer, and stop them in one conversation.&rdquo; Good. Just find out whether you&rsquo;re the one who has to start that conversation.</p>

